  "content": {
    "documentation_gaps": [
      "Team-level pre-treatment outcome series (≥6 equally spaced timepoints) for treated and control teams.",
      "Team-level usage logs for the AI tool (timestamps, feature counts, intensity metrics) linked to outcome windows.",
      "Quantitative measures of training exposure per team (attendance, hours, curriculum coverage) and any variation in training delivery.",
      "Records of how managers/teams were recruited and selected (application criteria, acceptance rules, selection logs).",
      "Time-stamped records of any concurrent HR/actions/incentives/deadlines or measurement-protocol changes around rollout.",
      "Sample sizes, cluster structure (teams within managers/divisions), and raw outcome data to compute standard errors and clustered inference.",
      "Attrition/missingness logs and team composition changes (hires, leaves, reassignments) during the study window.",
      "Any candidate instruments or rollout-assignment meta-data (encouragement randomization, timing variation, administrative cutoffs)."
    ],
    "epistemic_marker": "reasonable_hypothesis",
    "evidence_quality": 0.35,
    "factual_claims": [
      {
        "claim": "Treated teams show an 18% higher output at three months relative to teams without the tool.",
        "notes": "Stated in the scenario. No sampling variance, CIs, or raw data provided here—requires team-level data to confirm magnitude and precision.",
        "status": "established_fact"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Uptake was voluntary and treated teams also received special training (treatment bundle).",
        "notes": "Stated in the scenario. This creates two immediate empirical threats: selection on manager/team attributes and conflation of tool and training effects.",
        "status": "established_fact"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Selection bias and treatment-bundling are plausible explanations that can fully or partly generate the observed 18% difference absent any causal effect of the tool.",
        "notes": "Causal-inference principle: non-random uptake correlated with unobservables (motivation, manager ability) and an independent training effect can both inflate observed treated-control differences. Quantify via balance/pre-trend tests and sensitivity analysis to evaluate plausibility.",
        "status": "reasonable_hypothesis"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The consensus load-bearing assumption — that the CEO’s causal claim is therefore unjustified — can be decisively challenged if specific observable data patterns hold (balanced pre-trends, separability of training, within-treated dose–response, robust precision, and sensitivity bounds).",
        "notes": "Identification logic: if (a) pre-treatment trends and levels are closely balanced, (b) variation exists that separates tool exposure from training (or a valid instrument is present), (c) higher measured tool usage predicts larger post-treatment gains conditional on training, (d) the effect estimate is precise and robust to specifications, and (e) sensitivity analyses show implausibly large hidden confounders would be required to nullify the estimate — then the simplest and most plausible explanation becomes a causal effect of the tool. Each of these is testable on team-level data.",
        "status": "reasonable_hypothesis"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Concrete verification paths that would defeat the consensus objection are: (1) show no pre-treatment leads in an event-study (≥6 pre-periods), (2) demonstrate separable variation (tool-only vs training-only cells or a strong instrument with F>10 and placebo nulls), (3) find a monotonic dose–response of usage→outcome within treated teams controlling for training, and (4) run Oster/Rosenbaum-style bounds showing an implausibly large hidden bias would be needed to erase the effect.",
        "notes": "Operational checks: compute lead coefficients and joint tests; estimate first-stage F and covariate balance for candidate instruments; run within-treated fixed-effect regressions relating usage intensity to outcomes; report Oster δ or Rosenbaum Γ with observed-control explanatory power comparisons.",
        "status": "logical_inference"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Absent the data patterns above, the CEO's unqualified causal statement remains unjustified and a qualified descriptive statement is appropriate.",
        "notes": "If the key diagnostics cannot be computed (insufficient pre-periods; perfect collinearity of tool and training; no usage logs; tiny sample sizes), identification fails and causal attribution is not supportable.",
        "status": "logical_inference"
      }
    ],
    "target_section": "crucial_stress_test"
  }
}

